                </Vermiculite>
            </Main>
            <RURComments xml:space="preserve">
                <airSeal>Air sealing can provide significant energy savings with little investment. During the blower door test the following items were identified as large and easily repaired air leaks:



1.) Pellet stove chimney. The pellet stove is never used by the residents, but the chimney is still installed and unsealed. Applying an expanding spray foam around the chimney in the ceiling will prevent air infiltration around the chimney. Plugging the chimney itself with an air barrier (a plastic bag filled with fiberglass insulation, for example) will also reduce infiltration.



2.) Door replacement and weather stripping. Some of the solid wooden doors no longer seal in their frames well. The door on the Southeast side of the house was observed to be particularly leak prone, primarily due to a warp in the door that prevents it from setting in the frame. Adding or replacing weather stripping in the door frames will reduce air infiltration. Doors that are too warped to seal against the weather stripping may need to be replaced.</airSeal>
                <mainWalls>Adding insulation to exterior walls will result in significant savings. Adding wall insulation is often an expensive retrofit, but the Energy Solutions Center will reimburse part of that cost.</mainWalls>
                <windows>Replacing double pane, wood frame windows with triple pane, argon filled, low E coated vinyl frame windows will result in significant energy savings. The Energy Solutions Center offers reimbursement of $100 per window for this upgrade.</windows>
                <doors>Replacing wooden doors with spray foam insulated doors will reduce energy consumption. Replacing the doors will also likely reduce air infiltration.</doors>
            </RURComments>
        </Options>
